  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1216 PM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nSplit flow continues to dominate the long term forecast \nstarting Monday night. An upper level ridge wil be placed over \nus filtering down the colder air that was displaced from the \nArctic. A weak shortwave will also push through the region \nMonday night but unless you're on top of the Sierra madres and \npossibly adjacent valley's, I wouldn't get my hopes up about \nseeing snow any time soon. The global models (GFS,EURO,and \nCanadian) all seem too progressive with this ridge breaking down\nenough to let another system push into the Pacific NW and \npotentially giving us some chances for snow for the lower \nelevations. However, typically in a split flow regime the \nintermountain west usually gets nothing when we are smack dab in\nthe middle of it. THe lobes of vorticity are co- located with \nthe low pressure systems with almost no synoptic forcing over us\nto create precipitation with the exception of our mountain \npeaks. However, under the ridge the cold airmass will undergo \nsome modification and 700mb temperatures will have a slight \nincrease from -3 degree to a positive 3 degrees which means \nareas along and east of I-25 may expect to have temperatures \nreach into the 50's come Thanksgiving day and every shopper's \ncoveted \"holiday\" known as black friday.",
